      <p>Garage door work arrives in two very different moods. A spring snaps, the car is stuck inside, and the homeowner needs someone today. Or the old door looks tired and they start comparing styles and prices for a replacement. Google Ads can win both, but not with the same campaign.</p>

      <h2>Repairs, replacements and openers</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Emergency repairs:</strong> broken springs, cables, doors off track, doors that will not open or close. Urgent, call-driven, won by speed</li>
        <li><strong>New doors:</strong> higher value, more research, more comparison of styles and prices</li>
        <li><strong>Openers:</strong> installations and replacements, often with brand searches</li>
        <li><strong>Commercial doors</strong>, if you offer them, with their own buyers</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each gets its own ads, landing page, schedule and budget, so cheap repair clicks do not starve the replacement work, and replacement keywords do not bid like emergencies.</p>

      <h2>Trust wins the call</h2>
      <p>Garage door repair is a category where homeowners have learned to be careful. Lead sellers and bait-and-switch pricing have made people wary of ads that look too good to be true.</p>
      <p>Ads and landing pages that show you are a real local business earn more of the calls that become jobs: your business name, local address or service area, genuine reviews, warranties and honest pricing information. Specific, true claims beat a low headline price that the customer will not actually pay.</p>

      <h2>Speed for emergencies</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Emergency campaigns scheduled for the hours your phones are answered</li>
        <li>Call assets on every repair ad</li>
        <li>Same-day or response-time promises only where you can keep them</li>
        <li>Missed-call tracking, because an unanswered emergency is paid for and lost</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Measure booked jobs</h2>
      <p>A call that lasts a minute could be a customer, a supplier or someone asking for a part number. Counting every call teaches bidding to buy more calls, not more jobs. I qualify calls against your booking system and feed booked jobs, with their value, back to the campaigns that produced them, so a new door install counts for more than a spring adjustment. <a href="/learn/why-google-ads-calls-are-not-becoming-jobs/">Why your Google Ads calls are not becoming jobs</a> covers the usual gaps.</p>

      <h2>Where garage door budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>DIY searches: how to replace a spring, how to reset an opener</li>
        <li>Parts and spring purchase searches from people fixing it themselves</li>
        <li>Opener brand support searches, unless you service those brands</li>
        <li>Job searches and areas outside your service range</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The <a href="/tools/negative-keyword-generator/">negative keyword generator</a> covers the common groups, and you can add your own brands and areas.</p>

      <h2>Local Services Ads</h2>
      <p>Where they are available, Local Services Ads sit above everything else, charge per lead and show a Google verification badge. In a category where trust is the deciding factor, that badge helps. Use them alongside Search and measure them separately.</p>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>If you already advertise, the first question is whether the account counts booked jobs or just calls. A <a href="/google-ads-audit/">$200 account analysis</a> checks that before anything else. <a href="#enquiry">Ask for a free audit</a> and tell me the split you want between repairs and new doors.</p>
